{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01WtCdR5X6oPtkWHEcWQ2tmT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and determine what task to propose.\n\n## Step 1: Analyze project context\n\nThe project is `go-unix-utils` - a specification-driven pipeline that:\n1. Derives formal specs from C source code of classic Unix utilities\n2. Generates Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent\n3. Verifies parity against reference binaries through differential testing\n\n## Step 2: Summarize project state\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities have no formal specifications accessible to implementers. Re-implementations diverge in observable behavior.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/sys/` - Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions\n\n\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting (column alignment, ANSI color, human-readable sizes)\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `magefiles/` - Mage build targets\n- `docs/` - Specification and documentation suite\n\n**Current state**: All specs are complete for the planned releases, but the generated code hasn't been integrated into the working branch yet—only the previous generation outputs exist at tagged commits.\n\n**Current release**: rel01.0 (in_progress) with `rel01.0-uc001-ts` requiring `cmd/ts` implementation.\n\nI'm thinking through the dependency chain here. The timestamp utility depends on the differential testing harness, so ideally I'd implement `pkg/testutils` first, then `cmd/ts`. But I'm constrained to a single task, so I need to pick the one that unblocks the most work.\n\nSince nothing's been started yet and the working branch is empty, the foundational piece is `pkg/testutils` — without the testing harness, I can't verify any of the utilities. Looking at what needs to go into it: the test case data structure, binary execution logic, comparison and reporting, plus the normalization hooks for handling timestamp variations.\n\nThe planning rules are clear — split implementation from tests, keep tasks under 250 lines, and stick to exactly one task. So I'm proposing a single task to implement `pkg/testutils`. Following the naming convention, since `DiffTest` is the primary exported type, the main file should be `difftest.go` with its corresponding test file, and the `TimestampNormalizer` might live in a separate file. file like `normalizer.go`.\n\nLet me scope the task:\n\n**Task: Implement pkg/testutils - DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests (prd001 R1-R4)**\n\nFiles:\n- `pkg/testutils/difftest.go` - DiffTest struct, Normalize type, RunDiffTests function\n- `pkg/testutils/normalizer.go` - TimestampNormalizer, normalizer composition\n\nActually, per the planning constitution:\n- P2: \"Decompose each feature into up to three tasks in this order: (1) types and interfaces, (2) implementation, (3) tests.\"\n- \"A single task must never both implement and test the same feature.\"\n- \"If the types and interfaces are small enough to fit within the implementation task's line budget, combine steps 1 and 2 into one task.\"\n\nSince I can only create one task and need to follow the ordering rules, I should focus on the implementation task with types included, leaving tests for a future session. The task covers the DiffTest struct, RunDiffTests function with binary execution, comparison and reporting logic, and normalization hooks including TimestampNormalizer, while file state comparison can be deferred. be a separate task or can be included if it fits.\n\nLet me estimate line count:\n- DiffTest struct: ~30 lines\n- RunDiffTests: ~100 lines\n- TimestampNormalizer: ~30 lines  \n- Normalizer composition: ~20 lines\n- Total: ~180 lines\n\nThat fits within 150-250 lines. Good.\n\nShould I include R5 (file-state comparison)? That's maybe another 40-50 lines... bringing total to ~220-230 lines. Still within budget.\n\nLet me include R5 since it's also needed for sponge testing.\n\nNow I'm drafting the task description with the file structure and requirements. I'll need to cover the DiffTest struct implementation, the test execution logic with input/output capture, byte-for-byte comparison with divergence reporting, and the normalizer composition pattern for handling timestamp variations and other output differences. I'm finishing up the acceptance criteria—the normalizer needs to handle timestamp patterns and compose multiple normalization functions, file comparisons need byte-for-byte validation, execution must timeout after 10 seconds with a clear message, and the whole package should compile without errors. Now I'm structuring this as a formal YAML task definition with the implementation details and dependencies mapped out. I'm laying out the implementation plan with specific file structure and requirements. I need to define the core DiffTest struct with its fields for arguments, stdin, environment variables, exit codes, normalizers, and file expectations, then create a Normalize function type and FileExpectation struct to support comparison logic. The RunDiffTests function will execute each test case as a subtest, running both the reference and candidate binaries with identical inputs in isolated temporary directories, applying normalizers to handle dynamic content like timestamps before comparing outputs. I need to implement TimestampNormalizer to swap out timestamp patterns with a placeholder, and I'm organizing the code structure with difftest.go as the main file, normalizer.go for the timestamp logic, and using t.TempDir() to create isolated test directories for each binary invocation.\n\nThe diagnostic output needs to follow a specific order when tests fail—arguments first, then truncated stdin, followed by the reference and Go binary outputs for both stdout and stderr, finishing with exit codes. For the public API, I'm exporting DiffTest with all its fields, defining Normalize as a simple function type, creating FileExpectation for file assertions, and making RunDiffTests accept the testing.T parameter along with the test configuration.\n\nThe acceptance criteria also cover the test output behavior—passing tests should be silent while failures display the full diagnostic information—plus a TimestampNormalizer that masks timestamp patterns, a 10-second timeout for hanging binaries, and ensuring the package compiles cleanly without linting issues.\n\nNow I'm verifying the file structure: the primary exported type is `DiffTest`, so the main file should be `difftest.go` rather than `testutils.go`. The package lives at `github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/testutils` under the existing project module, and I don't need a separate `go.mod` since it's part of the same workspace. Let me check if there's already testutils code in the project that I should be aware of. I'm checking whether `Normalize` should be a type or function—the PRD specifies it as a function type, so it belongs in `difftest.go` with `DiffTest`. My task structure looks solid with 7 requirements, 8 acceptance criteria, and 4 design decisions, all within the target ranges, and I need to ensure the stitch agent has everything it needs to execute independently. Defining the struct fields and requirements...\n\nThe DiffTest struct needs Files as a slice of FileExpectation, and I'm laying out the remaining requirements around the RunDiffTests function—it should execute both binaries in subtests with a temporary directory for each test, enforce a 10-second timeout per invocation, and fail clearly if a binary doesn't exit in time.\n\nThen I need to apply any normalizer functions from the test to both stdout and stderr streams before comparing them byte-for-byte alongside exit codes, reporting detailed diagnostics on any mismatch. Finally, after both binaries finish, I'll read and compare the files specified in FileExpectation from both runs, flagging any content divergence.\n\nFor the timestamp normalizer specifically, I'm creating a function that finds patterns matching the standard timestamp format—like \"Feb 19 14:05:32\"—and replaces them with a placeholder so timestamps don't cause false failures. I'm also organizing the code so the primary test harness lives in difftest.go while normalizers get their own file to keep concerns separated.\n\nNow I'm thinking through how the diagnostic output should be structured when tests fail—it needs to emit the command arguments, stdin content, and the stdout/stderr from both the reference and Go implementations in a consistent order, along with their exit codes. The test harness will capture each binary's output independently into separate buffers so nothing leaks to the test process during normal runs, and I need to make sure the main exported functions and types are all accessible from the package level. the test process environment\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"A passing test run produces no output to the test process stdout or stderr\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"A failing stdout comparison produces a t.Fatalf message containing the triggering args, truncated stdin, both stdouts, both stderrs, and both exit codes\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"TimestampNormalizer replaces the string 'Feb 19 14:05:32' with 'TIMESTAMP' when applied to a byte slice containing that pattern\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"A binary that sleeps beyond the 10-second timeout causes the test to fail with a message containing the word 'timeout'\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"File-state comparison fails with a diagnostic when the Go binary writes different content than the reference binary to a named output file\"\n      - id: AC8\n        text: \"go build ./pkg/testutils/... succeeds with no compilation errors\"\n\nNow I'm checking that everything aligns properly—the requirements and acceptance criteria are in the right ranges, the design decisions are solid, and this foundational testutils package is the right starting point before building out the actual utilities.\n\nI should verify that the test suite already exists for this work, which it does at the test-rel01.0 spec, so I'm not violating the planning constitution. And since I'm only creating the implementation files and splitting tests into a separate task per the guidelines, the scope is clean and focused.\n\nNow I'm checking the YAML formatting to make sure all special characters are properly handled in the description block.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":73429,"cache_read_input_tokens":15559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":73429},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b09ffb17-3a38-4c1e-a0d4-e102e9020a5a","uuid":"a8baefab-0c80-4aeb-a57f-f4a008e3f202"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01WtCdR5X6oPtkWHEcWQ2tmT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Step 1–2: Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specs; re-implementations diverge from reference behavior in edge cases and flag interactions. This project derives specs from C source, generates Go implementations via an AI orchestrator, and verifies parity via differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: `cmd/<utility>/` holds per-utility Go binaries; `pkg/testutils/` is the shared differential test harness; `pkg/sys/` wraps Darwin/Linux syscalls; `pkg/format/` provides column layout, ANSI color, and human-readable sizes; `magefiles/` provides Mage targets; `docs/` holds all specs.\n\n**Current state**: All PRDs, use cases, and test suites are complete. The working branch has zero generated Go code. Prior generation runs live on archived `generation-merged` tags only.\n\n**Current release**: `rel01.0` — one use case: `rel01.0-uc001-ts` (in_progress, codestatus: not started). This requires `cmd/ts`, which in turn requires `pkg/testutils`.\n\n## Step 3: Priority Reasoning\n\nThe earliest incomplete release is rel01.0. Before `cmd/ts` can be implemented or verified, the differential testing harness (`pkg/testutils`) must exist — every utility's acceptance gate runs through it. Per canonical ordering, libraries before consumers. The implementation task comes before the test task (separate sessions). This is the single highest-priority unblocked task.\n\n## Step 4: Proposed Task\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement pkg/testutils differential testing harness (prd001 R1-R5)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/testutils/difftest.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"DiffTest struct, Normalize type, FileExpectation struct, RunDiffTests function, comparison and reporting logic\"\n      - path: pkg/testutils/normalizer.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"TimestampNormalizer implementation\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Define exported Normalize type as func([]byte) []byte and exported FileExpectation struct with Path (string) and Content ([]byte) fields, per prd001 R1.4 and R5.1\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Define exported DiffTest struct with fields Args ([]string), Stdin ([]byte, nil means no stdin -- EOF immediately), Env ([]string, nil means inherit test process environment), ExpectedExitCode (int), Normalizers ([]Normalize), and Files ([]FileExpectation), per prd001 R1.1-R1.4 and R5.1\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement exported RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBin, refBin string, tests []DiffTest) that runs each DiffTest as a named t.Run subtest, invoking both binaries with identical Args, Stdin, Env, and a per-test temporary working directory created by t.TempDir(), per prd001 R2.1-R2.5 and R3.6\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Enforce a 10-second default timeout on each binary invocation using exec.CommandContext with context.WithTimeout; a binary that does not exit within the timeout must fail the test with a clear timeout message, per prd001 R2.3\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Apply each function in DiffTest.Normalizers in slice order to both binaries stdout and stderr before comparison; compare normalized stdout, normalized stderr, and exit code byte-for-byte; call t.Fatalf on any divergence with a diagnostic including args, stdin truncated to 256 bytes, reference stdout, Go binary stdout, reference stderr, Go binary stderr, and both exit codes, per prd001 R3.1-R3.6 and R4.1-R4.3\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"After both binaries exit, for each FileExpectation in DiffTest.Files read the file at the given path relative to each binary's working directory and compare content byte-for-byte; report divergence with the file path and both file contents, per prd001 R5.1-R5.2\"\n      - id: R7\n        text: \"Implement exported TimestampNormalizer as a Normalize function that replaces substrings matching the default ts timestamp pattern (three-letter month abbreviation, space-padded day, HH:MM:SS -- e.g. 'Feb 19 14:05:32') with the fixed placeholder string 'TIMESTAMP', per prd001 R4.2\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Name the primary source file difftest.go after the primary exported type DiffTest; testutils.go is forbidden per project naming convention (P7)\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"TimestampNormalizer lives in normalizer.go to keep the normalizer registry separate from the execution harness in difftest.go\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"The diagnostic failure message emits fields in this fixed order: args, stdin (truncated to 256 bytes), reference binary stdout, Go binary stdout, reference binary stderr, Go binary stderr, reference exit code, Go binary exit code, per prd001 R3.5\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Each binary invocation captures stdout and stderr independently into separate bytes.Buffer values; neither buffer is written to the test process stdout or stderr during a passing run, per prd001 R2.4\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"DiffTest, Normalize, FileExpectation, and RunDiffTests are all exported from package testutils\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"A DiffTest with nil Stdin causes the binary to receive immediate EOF; a DiffTest with nil Env causes the binary to inherit the test process environment\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"A passing test run produces no output to the test process stdout or stderr\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"A failing stdout comparison calls t.Fatalf with a message containing the triggering args, truncated stdin, both stdouts, both stderrs, and both exit codes\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"TimestampNormalizer replaces the pattern 'Feb 19 14:05:32' with 'TIMESTAMP' when applied to a byte slice containing that string\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"A binary invocation that exceeds 10 seconds causes the test to fail with a message containing the word 'timeout'\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"File-state comparison fails with a diagnostic when the Go binary writes different content than the reference binary to a named output file path\"\n      - id: AC8\n        text: \"go build ./pkg/testutils/... succeeds with no compilation errors\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":73429,"cache_read_input_tokens":15559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":73429},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b09ffb17-3a38-4c1e-a0d4-e102e9020a5a","uuid":"c9307323-2ef4-4039-b703-8b514e8c12ca"}
